    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d655">

                <pb facs="0769" n="709" sameAs="frus:frus1895p1/pg709"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Sir
                            Julian Pauncefote</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-10-31"><hi rendition="#italic">October 31,
                        1895</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of the 26th instant in relation to the tax levied on United
                    States vessels entering and clearing from Canadian ports, which you observe is
                    still under consideration, and that it will receive prompt attention.</p>
                <pb facs="0770" n="710" xml:id="pg710"/>
                <p>I have received a letter from the Treasury Department of the 29th instant making
                    inquiry touching the subject, and adding that the season of lake navigation is
                    nearly ended for the year.</p>
                <p>In view of this circumstance and of your present announcement, permit me to
                    express the hope that the Department may, at an early date, be acquainted with
                    the decision of Her Majesty’s Government upon the subject after it shall have
                    been placed in possession of the report from the Canadian authorities referred
                    to in Lord Gough’s note of September 19, 1895.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
